"""NHSBSA dental activity history — what practices actually delivered (ledger 4.1).

Our own availability history began accruing today, so it is one night deep. This
is the escape hatch: NHSBSA has published, at **individual dental contract level
and monthly**, what every NHS dental contract in England actually *delivered* —
UDAs and FP17 courses of treatment — since April 2016. That is a decade of
longitudinal truth available in one afternoon, and it records what practices did
rather than what they claim. It is the supply side of the 4.2 model.

Source
------
NHSBSA Open Data Portal (CKAN), dataset
`english-contractor-monthly-general-dental-activity`_ — **Open Government Licence
v3.0**, no API key, no registration:

    https://opendata.nhsbsa.net/api/3/action/package_show
        ?id=english-contractor-monthly-general-dental-activity

Each monthly CSV is a separate CKAN resource; the download URL 302s to a
presigned object-store link, which ``PoliteClient`` follows transparently. Total
payload for the full history is ~128 MB across 121 files, so **nothing is capped
— the whole 2016-04 → present series is pulled** at 1 request/second.

.. _english-contractor-monthly-general-dental-activity:
   https://opendata.nhsbsa.net/dataset/english-contractor-monthly-general-dental-activity

Granularity actually obtained, and its limits — stated plainly
--------------------------------------------------------------
* **Contract-month.** One row per ``CONTRACT_NUMBER`` per ``YEAR_MONTH``. This is
  the finest granularity NHSBSA publishes openly. It is *not* site level: a
  contract may cover several surgery locations, and a location may host more than
  one contract. NHSBSA says so explicitly. Treating a contract as a practice is a
  simplification the 4.2 model must carry as a known error term.
* **General dental services only.** Orthodontic-only activity lives in a separate
  dataset (`english-contractor-monthly-orthodontic-activity`) and is *not* here.
  Locally commissioned dental services are in neither. National totals below are
  therefore GDS totals, which is exactly what the published statistics count.
* **Two-month reporting lag.** Dentists have 62 days to submit an FP17, so a month
  is published ~2 months after it closes. The most recent month present will
  always trail the calendar by about that much, and the newest one or two months
  can still revise upward as late claims land.
* **England only.** Commissioners are English ICBs; there is no Welsh, Scottish or
  Northern Irish content to filter out.
* **Band 2 changed shape mid-series.** From 25 November 2022 band 2 was split into
  sub-bands 2a/2b/2c and the flat ``BAND_2_DELIVERED`` column was retired. Both
  layouts are parsed; ``band_2`` is the sum across whichever sub-bands the file
  carries, so the series is continuous.
* **Patient counts are rolling, not monthly.** ``CHILD_12M_COUNT`` and
  ``ADULT_24M_COUNT`` are unique patients seen in the trailing 12 / 24 months as
  at that month. They are carried through unchanged and are **never summed across
  months** — doing so would multiply-count the same patients. ``summarise`` only
  ever reports them for a single month.

Reconciliation against published statistics
-------------------------------------------
Totals are checked against NHSBSA's own *NHS Dental Statistics for England
2024/25* (published 28 August 2025, updated 27 March 2026), Table 4 "Number of
units of dental activity (UDA) in England 2019/20 to 2024/25":

    2019/20  79,725,106      2022/23  70,130,676
    2020/21  24,351,462      2023/24  72,502,208
    2021/22  57,723,637      2024/25  73,088,476

``reconcile()`` reports our financial-year totals beside those figures. Anything
outside a couple of percent means double-counting or the wrong dataset, and
should be investigated rather than published.

The join key problem — read this before step 3.3
-------------------------------------------------
**This dataset carries no ODS code.** Its entity key is ``CONTRACT_NUMBER``, a
10-digit NHSBSA contract identifier that has no published crosswalk to the
``V#####`` ODS codes in ``data/dist/ods_practices.parquet`` or to nhs.uk profile
IDs. What it does carry is ``LATEST_PPC_ADDRESS_POSTCODE`` and ``LSOA11_CODE``,
so geography is the only bridge open to us without a licensed lookup.
``join_report()`` measures — it does not resolve — how far postcode alone gets us,
including how often the relationship is many-to-many. Building the resolution is
step 3.3 and deliberately does not happen here.

Raw CSVs go through the same append-only store as nightly snapshots, so the pull
is reproducible and re-parseable without touching the network again.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import asyncio
import csv
import io
import json
import re
import sys
from collections import defaultdict
from dataclasses import asdict, dataclass
from pathlib import Path

from client import PoliteClient
from snapshot import Record, SnapshotWriter, iter_payloads, latest_day, utc_today

PORTAL = "https://opendata.nhsbsa.net"
PACKAGE = "english-contractor-monthly-general-dental-activity"
PACKAGE_SHOW = f"{PORTAL}/api/3/action/package_show?id={PACKAGE}"

BSA_RATE = 1.0  # ~128 MB over 121 files; there is nothing to gain from hurrying
DEFAULT_STORE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent / "data" / "reference" / "bsa"

# A resource is a month of contract activity only if it carries both of these.
# Matching on columns rather than on the file name survives NHSBSA renaming the
# rolling "latest month" resource, which they do on every release.
REQUIRED_COLUMNS = ("YEAR_MONTH", "CONTRACT_NUMBER", "UDA_DELIVERED")

BAND_COLUMNS = (
    "BAND_1_DELIVERED",
    "BAND_2_DELIVERED",
    "BAND_2A_DELIVERED",
    "BAND_2B_DELIVERED",
    "BAND_2C_DELIVERED",
    "BAND_3_DELIVERED",
    "BAND_URGENT_DELIVERED",
    "BAND_OTHER_DELIVERED",
)

# NHS Dental Statistics for England 2024/25, Table 4. Our own financial-year
# totals are reported against these; see the module docstring for the citation.
PUBLISHED_UDA = {
    "2019-20": 79_725_106,
    "2020-21": 24_351_462,
    "2021-22": 57_723_637,
    "2022-23": 70_130_676,
    "2023-24": 72_502_208,
    "2024-25": 73_088_476,
}
# A financial year we agree with to within this is reconciled. The open data is
# the same warehouse the statistics are cut from, so the gap should be tiny; the
# tolerance exists for revisions landing between the two publications.
RECONCILE_TOLERANCE = 0.02

# Guardrails. England ran ~6,500-7,500 GDS contracts a month across the series and
# delivers 5-7m UDAs in a normal month; COVID year 2020/21 is the one real outlier.
MIN_EXPECTED_MONTHS = 24
MIN_EXPECTED_CONTRACTS = 5_000
MIN_MONTHLY_UDA = 500_000
MAX_MONTHLY_UDA = 12_000_000

_YEAR_MONTH = re.compile(r"^(\d{4})(\d{2})$")


@dataclass(slots=True)
class ContractMonth:
    """One NHS dental contract's delivered activity in one treatment month."""

    year_month: str  # "YYYYMM", the month treatment completed
    financial_year: str  # "2016-17"
    contract_number: str
    provider_name: str
    commissioner_code: str
    commissioner_name: str
    postcode: str  # LATEST_PPC_ADDRESS_POSTCODE, normalised (no spaces, upper)
    lsoa11_code: str
    uda_target: float | None  # contracted UDAs for the year, not the month
    uda_financial_value: float | None
    uda_delivered: float
    uda_delivered_fd: float  # by foundation dentists; excluded from uda_delivered
    late_submitted_fp17: float
    band_1: float
    band_2: float  # flat band 2 pre-2022, else 2a+2b+2c
    band_3: float
    band_urgent: float
    band_other: float
    child_12m_count: float  # rolling — never sum across months
    adult_24m_count: float  # rolling — never sum across months

    @property
    def cot(self) -> float:
        """FP17 courses of treatment delivered — the sum of the bands."""
        return self.band_1 + self.band_2 + self.band_3 + self.band_urgent + self.band_other

    @property
    def key(self) -> tuple[str, str]:
        return (self.year_month, self.contract_number)


# -- period helpers --------------------------------------------------------


def financial_year(year_month: str) -> str:
    """"202603" -> "2025-26". April starts the NHS financial year."""
    m = _YEAR_MONTH.match(year_month)
    if not m:
        return ""
    year, month = int(m.group(1)), int(m.group(2))
    if not 1 <= month <= 12:
        return ""
    start = year if month >= 4 else year - 1
    return f"{start}-{(start + 1) % 100:02d}"


def _norm_postcode(value: str) -> str:
    """Match the ODS register's own normalisation so a join is even possible."""
    return "".join(value.split()).upper()


def _num(value: str | None) -> float:
    """Blank means "no activity", not "unknown" — a contract with no FP17s in a
    month is published with empty cells, and it really did deliver zero."""
    if value is None:
        return 0.0
    value = value.strip().replace(",", "")
    if not value:
        return 0.0
    try:
        return float(value)
    except ValueError:
        return 0.0


def _opt_num(value: str | None) -> float | None:
    if value is None or not value.strip():
        return None
    try:
        return float(value.strip().replace(",", ""))
    except ValueError:
        return None


# -- the resource catalogue ------------------------------------------------


def monthly_resources(package: dict) -> list[dict]:
    """CSV resources that are a month of contract activity, in publication order.

    The package also carries data dictionaries and, on every release, a rolling
    "latest month" resource under a *different* name. Selecting on the column set
    picks both up and ignores the dictionaries; the name pattern is the fallback
    for the 23 resources whose schema CKAN stored unparseably (see
    ``resource_columns``).
    """
    result = package.get("result") or package
    out = []
    for resource in result.get("resources") or []:
        if (resource.get("format") or "").upper() != "CSV":
            continue
        columns = resource_columns(resource)
        if columns:
            if not all(c in columns for c in REQUIRED_COLUMNS):
                continue
        elif not _RESOURCE_NAME.match((resource.get("name") or "").strip().upper()):
            continue
        out.append(resource)
    out.sort(key=lambda r: (r.get("position") or 0))
    return out


def resource_columns(resource: dict) -> set[str]:
    """Declared column names for a CKAN resource, or an empty set if unreadable.

    CKAN serves this package's schema inconsistently: most resources carry a JSON
    object, but a run of 23 (2023-04 to 2025-02) carry a **Python repr string**
    — ``"{u'fields': [{u'name': u'YEAR_MONTH', ...}]}"`` — which is not JSON and
    cannot be parsed as such. Filtering on ``isinstance(schema, dict)`` silently
    dropped 23 months of history, so both encodings are read.
    """
    schema = resource.get("schema")
    if isinstance(schema, dict):
        return {(f.get("name") or "").strip().upper() for f in schema.get("fields") or []}
    if isinstance(schema, str) and schema.strip():
        return {m.upper() for m in _REPR_FIELD_NAME.findall(schema)}
    return set()


# -- fetch -----------------------------------------------------------------


async def fetch_activity(
    *,
    store: Path | str = DEFAULT_STORE,
    day: str | None = None,
    rate: float = BSA_RATE,
    months: int | None = None,
) -> int:
    """Pull the CKAN package and every monthly CSV. Returns an exit code."""
    day = day or utc_today()

    async with PoliteClient(rate_per_sec=rate, concurrency=1, timeout=120.0) as client:
        meta = await client.get(PACKAGE_SHOW)
        if not meta.ok:
            print(f"FATAL: package_show failed ({meta.status})", file=sys.stderr)
            return 1
        try:
            package = json.loads(meta.body)
        except json.JSONDecodeError:
            print("FATAL: package_show did not return JSON", file=sys.stderr)
            return 1

        resources = monthly_resources(package)
        if not resources:
            print(
                "FATAL: no monthly contract-activity resources in the package — "
                "the column set has changed and the selector needs revisiting.",
                file=sys.stderr,
            )
            return 1
        if months:
            resources = resources[-months:]

        with SnapshotWriter(root=store, day=day, route="nhsbsa-uda-contractor") as writer:
            writer.add(
                Record(
                    practice_id="package",
                    url=PACKAGE_SHOW,
                    fetched_at=meta.fetched_at,
                    status=meta.status,
                    body=meta.body,
                    content_type=meta.content_type,
                    meta={"kind": "package", "resources": len(resources)},
                )
            )
            print(f"Pulling {len(resources)} monthly contract-activity files", flush=True)

            for i, resource in enumerate(resources):
                url = resource.get("url") or ""
                name = resource.get("name") or f"resource-{i}"
                result = await client.get(url)
                if not result.ok:
                    print(f"FATAL: {name} failed ({result.status})", file=sys.stderr)
                    return 1
                writer.add(
                    Record(
                        practice_id=name,
                        url=url,
                        fetched_at=result.fetched_at,
                        status=result.status,
                        body=result.body,
                        content_type=result.content_type,
                        meta={"kind": "month", "position": resource.get("position"), "name": name},
                    )
                )
                if (i + 1) % 10 == 0 or i + 1 == len(resources):
                    print(f"  {i + 1}/{len(resources)} files", flush=True)
    return 0


# -- parse -----------------------------------------------------------------


def parse_csv(body: bytes) -> list[ContractMonth]:
    """Parse one monthly CSV. Headers carry stray whitespace in some vintages."""
    reader = csv.reader(io.StringIO(body.decode("utf-8-sig")))
    try:
        header = [h.strip().upper() for h in next(reader)]
    except StopIteration:
        return []
    if not all(c in header for c in REQUIRED_COLUMNS):
        return []
    index = {name: i for i, name in enumerate(header)}
    bands = [b for b in BAND_COLUMNS if b in index]
    # Repeated across thousands of rows; interning keeps a decade in memory.
    pool: dict[str, str] = {}

    def cell(row: list[str], name: str) -> str:
        i = index.get(name)
        return row[i].strip() if i is not None and i < len(row) else ""

    def shared(value: str) -> str:
        return pool.setdefault(value, value)

    out: list[ContractMonth] = []
    for row in reader:
        year_month = cell(row, "YEAR_MONTH")
        contract = cell(row, "CONTRACT_NUMBER")
        if not _YEAR_MONTH.match(year_month) or not contract:
            continue
        band = {b: _num(cell(row, b)) for b in bands}
        out.append(
            ContractMonth(
                year_month=shared(year_month),
                financial_year=shared(financial_year(year_month)),
                contract_number=contract,
                provider_name=shared(cell(row, "PROVIDER_NAME")),
                commissioner_code=shared(cell(row, "COMMISSIONER_CODE")),
                commissioner_name=shared(cell(row, "COMMISSIONER_NAME")),
                postcode=shared(_norm_postcode(cell(row, "LATEST_PPC_ADDRESS_POSTCODE"))),
                lsoa11_code=shared(cell(row, "LSOA11_CODE")),
                uda_target=_opt_num(cell(row, "UDA_PERF_TARGET")),
                uda_financial_value=_opt_num(cell(row, "UDA_FIN_VAL")),
                uda_delivered=_num(cell(row, "UDA_DELIVERED")),
                uda_delivered_fd=_num(cell(row, "UDA_DELIVERED_FD")),
                late_submitted_fp17=_num(cell(row, "LATE_SUBMITTED_FP17")),
                band_1=band.get("BAND_1_DELIVERED", 0.0),
                # Band 2 was split into 2a/2b/2c from 25 Nov 2022; only one of the
                # two layouts is ever present, so adding all four is safe.
                band_2=(
                    band.get("BAND_2_DELIVERED", 0.0)
                    + band.get("BAND_2A_DELIVERED", 0.0)
                    + band.get("BAND_2B_DELIVERED", 0.0)
                    + band.get("BAND_2C_DELIVERED", 0.0)
                ),
                band_3=band.get("BAND_3_DELIVERED", 0.0),
                band_urgent=band.get("BAND_URGENT_DELIVERED", 0.0),
                band_other=band.get("BAND_OTHER_DELIVERED", 0.0),
                child_12m_count=_num(cell(row, "CHILD_12M_COUNT")),
                adult_24m_count=_num(cell(row, "ADULT_24M_COUNT")),
            )
        )
    return out


def load_activity(
    store: Path | str = DEFAULT_STORE,
    day: str | None = None,
) -> list[ContractMonth]:
    """Read a stored pull into normalised contract-month rows.

    Files are read in capture order and de-duplicated on (month, contract): the
    rolling "latest month" resource overlaps the fixed monthly files whenever a
    month graduates from one to the other, and a later file is the revised one.
    """
    store = Path(store)
    day = day or latest_day(store)
    if not day:
        raise FileNotFoundError(f"no NHSBSA pull found under {store}")

    rows: dict[tuple[str, str], ContractMonth] = {}
    for entry, body in iter_payloads(store, day):
        if (entry.get("meta") or {}).get("kind") == "package":
            continue
        for row in parse_csv(body):
            rows[row.key] = row
    return sorted(rows.values(), key=lambda r: (r.year_month, r.contract_number))


# -- summary and reconciliation --------------------------------------------


def summarise(rows: list[ContractMonth]) -> dict:
    """Coverage and totals — published per constraint C8.

    Rolling patient counts are reported for the latest month only. Summing them
    across months would count the same patient once per month they appear in.
    """
    months = sorted({r.year_month for r in rows})
    by_year: dict[str, dict] = {}
    for row in rows:
        year = by_year.setdefault(
            row.financial_year,
            {"uda": 0.0, "uda_fd": 0.0, "cot": 0.0, "months": set(), "contracts": set()},
        )
        year["uda"] += row.uda_delivered
        year["uda_fd"] += row.uda_delivered_fd
        year["cot"] += row.cot
        year["months"].add(row.year_month)
        year["contracts"].add(row.contract_number)

    latest = months[-1] if months else ""
    latest_rows = [r for r in rows if r.year_month == latest]
    return {
        "rows": len(rows),
        "months": len(months),
        "first_month": months[0] if months else "",
        "last_month": latest,
        "missing_months": _missing_months(months),
        "financial_years": {
            year: {
                "uda_delivered": round(v["uda"], 2),
                "uda_delivered_fd": round(v["uda_fd"], 2),
                "cot": round(v["cot"], 2),
                "months": len(v["months"]),
                "contracts": len(v["contracts"]),
                "complete": len(v["months"]) == 12,
            }
            for year, v in sorted(by_year.items())
        },
        "contracts": len({r.contract_number for r in rows}),
        "commissioners": len({r.commissioner_code for r in rows if r.commissioner_code}),
        "postcodes": len({r.postcode for r in rows if r.postcode}),
        "rows_without_postcode": sum(1 for r in rows if not r.postcode),
        "rows_without_lsoa": sum(1 for r in rows if not r.lsoa11_code),
        "total_uda_delivered": round(sum(r.uda_delivered for r in rows), 2),
        "total_uda_delivered_fd": round(sum(r.uda_delivered_fd for r in rows), 2),
        "total_cot": round(sum(r.cot for r in rows), 2),
        "latest_month_contracts": len(latest_rows),
        "latest_month_child_12m": round(sum(r.child_12m_count for r in latest_rows), 0),
        "latest_month_adult_24m": round(sum(r.adult_24m_count for r in latest_rows), 0),
    }


def _missing_months(months: list[str]) -> list[str]:
    """Gaps in an otherwise contiguous series. A hole here is a lost file, not a
    month England did no dentistry in."""
    if len(months) < 2:
        return []

    def ordinal(ym: str) -> int:
        return int(ym[:4]) * 12 + int(ym[4:]) - 1

    present = {ordinal(m) for m in months}
    return [
        f"{o // 12:04d}{o % 12 + 1:02d}"
        for o in range(min(present), max(present) + 1)
        if o not in present
    ]


def reconcile(stats: dict, published: dict[str, int] | None = None) -> list[dict]:
    """Compare our financial-year UDA totals with the published statistics.

    Only complete (12-month) years are judged: a part year is legitimately short
    and flagging it would train us to ignore the check.
    """
    published = PUBLISHED_UDA if published is None else published
    out = []
    for year, figure in sorted(published.items()):
        ours = stats["financial_years"].get(year)
        if not ours:
            out.append({"financial_year": year, "published": figure, "status": "absent"})
            continue
        total = ours["uda_delivered"]
        diff = total - figure
        pct = diff / figure if figure else 0.0
        if not ours["complete"]:
            status = "partial"
        elif abs(pct) <= RECONCILE_TOLERANCE:
            status = "ok"
        else:
            status = "MISMATCH"
        out.append(
            {
                "financial_year": year,
                "published": figure,
                "ours": total,
                "difference": round(diff, 2),
                "pct": pct,
                "complete": ours["complete"],
                "status": status,
            }
        )
    return out


def check_plausible(stats: dict) -> list[str]:
    """Guardrails against publishing a supply series we know is wrong."""
    problems: list[str] = []
    if stats["months"] < MIN_EXPECTED_MONTHS:
        problems.append(f"only {stats['months']} months captured (expected at least {MIN_EXPECTED_MONTHS})")
    if stats["missing_months"]:
        problems.append(f"gaps in the monthly series: {', '.join(stats['missing_months'][:6])}")
    if stats["contracts"] < MIN_EXPECTED_CONTRACTS:
        problems.append(
            f"only {stats['contracts']:,} distinct contracts "
            f"(expected at least {MIN_EXPECTED_CONTRACTS:,})"
        )
    if stats["months"]:
        per_month = stats["total_uda_delivered"] / stats["months"]
        if not MIN_MONTHLY_UDA <= per_month <= MAX_MONTHLY_UDA:
            problems.append(
                f"mean {per_month:,.0f} UDAs per month is outside the plausible "
                f"range {MIN_MONTHLY_UDA:,}-{MAX_MONTHLY_UDA:,} — likely double-counted"
            )
    return problems


# -- the join key question (measured for step 3.3, not solved here) ---------


def contract_directory(rows: list[ContractMonth]) -> dict[str, ContractMonth]:
    """One row per contract, taking its most recent month.

    The postcode column is documented as the *latest* practice address, so the
    most recent month is the only defensible representative of a contract.
    """
    latest: dict[str, ContractMonth] = {}
    for row in rows:
        current = latest.get(row.contract_number)
        if current is None or row.year_month > current.year_month:
            latest[row.contract_number] = row
    return latest


def join_report(
    directory: dict[str, ContractMonth],
    ods_postcodes: dict[str, list[str]],
    *,
    active_since: str = "",
) -> dict:
    """Measure — do not resolve — how far postcode alone links BSA to ODS.

    This dataset has no ODS code and no published crosswalk, so postcode is the
    only bridge available without a licensed lookup. Step 3.3 owns the actual
    resolution; all this does is report honestly how much of it postcode can do
    and where it is ambiguous.

    ``active_since`` optionally restricts the measurement to contracts still
    delivering in or after that month, because a contract that ended in 2018 has
    no reason to appear in today's ODS register and would depress the rate for
    the wrong reason.
    """
    contracts = [
        c for c in directory.values() if not active_since or c.year_month >= active_since
    ]
    with_postcode = [c for c in contracts if c.postcode]
    matched = [c for c in with_postcode if c.postcode in ods_postcodes]

    contracts_per_postcode: dict[str, int] = defaultdict(int)
    for c in matched:
        contracts_per_postcode[c.postcode] += 1

    one_to_one = sum(
        1
        for c in matched
        if len(ods_postcodes[c.postcode]) == 1 and contracts_per_postcode[c.postcode] == 1
    )
    many_ods = sum(1 for c in matched if len(ods_postcodes[c.postcode]) > 1)
    many_contracts = sum(1 for c in matched if contracts_per_postcode[c.postcode] > 1)

    ods_matched = {code for c in matched for code in ods_postcodes[c.postcode]}
    total_ods = len({code for codes in ods_postcodes.values() for code in codes})

    return {
        "contracts": len(contracts),
        "with_postcode": len(with_postcode),
        "matched": len(matched),
        "unmatched": len(with_postcode) - len(matched),
        "match_rate": len(matched) / len(contracts) if contracts else 0.0,
        "one_to_one": one_to_one,
        "one_to_one_rate": one_to_one / len(matched) if matched else 0.0,
        "postcode_has_many_ods": many_ods,
        "postcode_has_many_contracts": many_contracts,
        "ods_practices_reached": len(ods_matched),
        "ods_practices_total": total_ods,
        "ods_coverage": len(ods_matched) / total_ods if total_ods else 0.0,
        "unmatched_sample": sorted(
            {c.postcode for c in with_postcode if c.postcode not in ods_postcodes}
        )[:10],
    }


def ods_postcode_index(path: Path | str) -> dict[str, list[str]]:
    """Active ODS dental practices keyed by normalised postcode, or {} if absent."""
    path = Path(path)
    if not path.exists():
        return {}
    import polars as pl

    index: dict[str, list[str]] = defaultdict(list)
    frame = pl.read_parquet(path)
    for org_id, postcode in zip(
        frame["org_id"].to_list(), frame["postcode"].to_list(), strict=False
    ):
        key = _norm_postcode(postcode or "")
        if key:
            index[key].append(org_id)
    return dict(index)


# -- output ----------------------------------------------------------------


def write_parquet(rows: list[ContractMonth], out_path: Path | str) -> Path:
    import polars as pl

    out_path = Path(out_path)
    out_path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    pl.DataFrame([{**asdict(r), "cot": r.cot} for r in rows]).write_parquet(
        out_path, compression="zstd"
    )
    return out_path


def main(argv: list[str] | None = None) -> int:
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(description="NHSBSA dental activity history (contract-month).")
    ap.add_argument("--store", default=str(DEFAULT_STORE))
    ap.add_argument("--day", default=None)
    ap.add_argument("--out", default="data/dist/bsa_activity.parquet")
    ap.add_argument("--ods", default="data/dist/ods_practices.parquet")
    ap.add_argument("--months", type=int, default=None, help="cap to the N most recent months")
    ap.add_argument("--skip-fetch", action="store_true", help="parse an existing pull only")
    args = ap.parse_args(argv)

    if not args.skip_fetch:
        code = asyncio.run(fetch_activity(store=args.store, day=args.day, months=args.months))
        if code:
            return code

    rows = load_activity(args.store, args.day)
    stats = summarise(rows)

    print(f"\nContract-months: {stats['rows']:,}")
    print(f"Period: {stats['first_month']} to {stats['last_month']} ({stats['months']} months)")
    print(f"Distinct contracts: {stats['contracts']:,}")
    print(f"Distinct commissioners: {stats['commissioners']:,}")
    print(f"Distinct practice postcodes: {stats['postcodes']:,}")
    print(f"Total UDAs delivered: {stats['total_uda_delivered']:,.0f}")
    print(f"  by foundation dentists (separate column): {stats['total_uda_delivered_fd']:,.0f}")
    print(f"Total FP17 courses of treatment: {stats['total_cot']:,.0f}")
    if stats["rows_without_postcode"]:
        print(f"Rows with no postcode: {stats['rows_without_postcode']:,}")

    print("\nFinancial year          UDAs delivered           COTs   contracts")
    for year, v in stats["financial_years"].items():
        flag = "" if v["complete"] else f"  ({v['months']} months)"
        print(
            f"  {year}   {v['uda_delivered']:>18,.0f} {v['cot']:>14,.0f} "
            f"{v['contracts']:>11,}{flag}"
        )

    print("\nReconciliation vs NHS Dental Statistics for England 2024/25, Table 4:")
    mismatched = False
    for check in reconcile(stats):
        if check["status"] == "absent":
            print(f"  {check['financial_year']}  published {check['published']:>12,}   not captured")
            continue
        print(
            f"  {check['financial_year']}  published {check['published']:>12,}  "
            f"ours {check['ours']:>14,.0f}  {check['pct']:+.2%}  {check['status']}"
        )
        mismatched = mismatched or check["status"] == "MISMATCH"

    problems = check_plausible(stats)
    if problems:
        for problem in problems:
            print(f"FATAL: {problem}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    if mismatched:
        print(
            "FATAL: a complete financial year disagrees with the published total — "
            "investigate before publishing.",
            file=sys.stderr,
        )
        return 1

    ods = ods_postcode_index(args.ods)
    if ods:
        directory = contract_directory(rows)
        recent = join_report(directory, ods, active_since=stats["last_month"])
        print(
            f"\nJoin to ODS by postcode (contracts active in {stats['last_month']}): "
            f"{recent['matched']:,}/{recent['contracts']:,} ({recent['match_rate']:.1%})"
        )
        print(f"  unambiguous 1:1 postcode links: {recent['one_to_one']:,} ({recent['one_to_one_rate']:.1%})")
        print(f"  postcode holds >1 ODS practice: {recent['postcode_has_many_ods']:,}")
        print(f"  postcode holds >1 BSA contract: {recent['postcode_has_many_contracts']:,}")
        print(
            f"  ODS practices reached: {recent['ods_practices_reached']:,}/"
            f"{recent['ods_practices_total']:,} ({recent['ods_coverage']:.1%})"
        )
        print("  NOTE: measurement only — entity resolution is ledger step 3.3.")

    out = write_parquet(rows, args.out)
    print(f"\nWrote {len(rows):,} contract-month rows to {out}")
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    raise SystemExit(main())
